  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Short break in the muggy precip pattern today\n\nSat RGB composite shows the expected fog in the valleys. This\nshould dissipate by 8 or 9 AM in most places but could take\nuntil 10 AM in the deeper places. After that, only some higher\nclouds moving north out of the SE and isold cu, mainly over the\nNW. Temps not too different from Mon, just a shade warmer in the\nAllegheny Plateau and Laurel Highlands. RH dips into the 40s and\nlower 50s for everyone this aftn. \n\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warmer/more humid with heavy downpours Wed-Sat\n\nUpper low over GA will open up and get absorbed by the longer-\nwave trough moving across the nrn Plains and upper Great Lakes.\nThe resulting SSE flow of high PWAT air and lack of well-defined\nfronts and will put us back into a muggy and unsettled pattern.\nThe lack of organization will mean more pulse/airmass type\nthunderstorms. The high moisture content will make heavy\ndownpours likely. But, targeting where they will be on any\nspecific day is going to be too difficult at this range.\nRiver gauges already show a return to base flows in most places\nthat received the very heavy rainfall and flooding the other \nday. Thankfully, the coverage shouldn't be too dense Wed and \nThurs. The expected isolated/sct coverage should give the \nground a little more time to dry out. But, with each day, the\ncoverage should become more complete. Each successive day \nshould also yield a slightly higher worry for flooding. At this \npoint, the WPC outlooks remain in the next-to-lowest (marginal) \ncategory, just above no worries, for W,R,F & Sat which is almost\ncertainly a nod to the scattered nature of the expected\nairmass/pulse storms.",
